<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lý Thái Tổ</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/974">974</a>–1028</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Lý Công Uẩn, Ly Cong Uan, Ly Thai To</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lý Thái Tổ, born Lý Công Uẩn, was the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s Lý dynasty. Raised in a Buddhist monastery, he became a palace commander and was chosen as king in 1009 after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-long-dinh">Lê Long Đĩnh</a>, the last Early Lê ruler. In 1010 he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-lu">Hoa Lư</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-la">Đại La</a>, renaming it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>. He reigned until 1028.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ly-thai-to.jpg" alt="Lý Thái Tổ" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Whyte from Dublin, Ireland · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C6%B0%E1%BB%A3ng_L%C3%BD_Th%C3%A1i_T%E1%BB%95_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lý Công Uẩn was born on 8 March 974 in Cổ Pháp, in what is now Bắc Ninh Province. The chronicles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-viet-su-ky-toan-thu">Đại Việt sử ký toàn thư</a> and Đại Việt sử lược record his mother only by the surname Phạm, and his father is unknown; the chronicles say his mother conceived him after meeting a demigod at Tiêu Sơn temple. The historian Gregory Sattler notes that these records were written centuries later and that his family was already influential before his rise, a point later writers may have played down to stress his divine birth. He served in the army of the Anterior Lê dynasty and rose to Palace Commander.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The chronicles tell that a kapok tree struck by lightning in his home district bore a poem foretelling the fall of the Lê and the rise of the Lý. On taking the throne he judged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-lu">Hoa Lư</a>, the mountain fortress capital of the Đinh and Anterior Lê, too narrow and damp, and issued an edict moving the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-la">Đại La</a>, the city the Tang general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-pian">Gao Pian</a> had built in the 860s.</p><p>He replaced the ten circuits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Đinh Bộ Lĩnh</a> with 24 routes and organised the southern provinces as military outposts. In 1011 he led an army against rebels in what is now Thanh Hoá and Nghệ An, campaigning for two years and burning villages; a storm on his voyage home in 1012 he took as divine judgement for the violence. From 1013 to 1015 he sent troops against the Hani of the northern mountains, allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a>, and in 1013 he created six classes of tax.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> emperor recognised him in 1010 with the usual titles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>. Having begun life as a Buddhist monk, he promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, building eight temples in the Tiên Du area and three around the capital, and the Lý court brought the cults of regional spirits, among them the Trưng sisters, to temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 31 March 1028 and was buried at Thọ Lăng, the Mausoleum of Longevity. His dynasty ruled for 216 years. The historian Lê Văn Hưu judged that he fell short of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Lê Đại Hành</a> in war but surpassed him in winning the people's gratitude and founding a lasting dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> counts him among its 14 national heroes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lý Thái Tổ” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last ruler, Le Long Dinh, was remembered as cruel, and on his death in 1009 the court official <b>Ly Cong Uan</b> was chosen as king, founding the Ly dynasty, which moved the capital to Thang Long.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-le-dynasty">Early Le dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1009 the court official <b>Ly Cong Uan</b>, raised in a Buddhist monastery, was chosen to succeed the Early Le dynasty and reigned as Ly Thai To.</blockquote><a href="/state/ly-dynasty">Ly dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Từ Sơn, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.12,105.96&amp;year=1009" aria-label="Từ Sơn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7398 1383 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lý Thái Tổ"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7848 1653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7848 1653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.12,105.96&amp;year=1009">What was here in 1009? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
